//! **Regression: a big `&str` constant must not become a hard error under verification.**
//!
//! `trust-thir-lower` asks rustc for the VALTREE of every named constant it
//! lowers (`const_eval_resolve_for_typeck`). A `&str` valtree has one leaf per
//! byte, and rustc caps a valtree at `VALTREE_MAX_NODES` (100_000), emitting
//! `maximum number of nodes exceeded in constant …` as a HARD ERROR inside the
//! query — so every caller's graceful `Err` handling is too late. A stock build
//! never valtrees such a constant, so a crate that compiles fine with rustc
//! failed to compile under Trust in ANY policy.
//!
//! MEASURED 2026-09-08 on `first-party/ty`: `targo trust check -p tla-cli` walked
//! all 316 dependency units clean and died on the final crate at
//! `const ENV_OPTIONS_JSON: &str = include_str!("../options/env_options.json")`
//! — 149_839 bytes, referenced as `serde_json::from_str(ENV_OPTIONS_JSON)`.
//!
//! The fix: the `eval_to_valtree` QUERY is silent — the hard error is emitted by
//! rustc's `const_eval_global_id_for_typeck` wrapper on `NodesOverflow` — so
//! Trust asks the query directly (`quiet_const_valtree`) and treats overflow
//! like any other non-valtree'able constant: decline to the opaque lane, which
//! is a completeness cost — the call keeps its honest obligation — never a
//! build error. `build-pass` under advisory is the assertion; the strict lane
//! then judges `count(BIG)`'s obligation on its own merits.

